<<<
See -EN VERBS. lama. See LAMA. -LL-, -L-. Final / is treated
===
See ^^-EN VERBS^^.

**llama.** See ^^LAMA^^.

**-LL-, -L-.** Final *l* is treated
>>>

<<<
usage from most final consonants, the rule being to double it, if single, in inflexions & in some derivatives irrespective of the position of the accent, 1. When verbs in -1 (except those in which a compound vowel sound, as
===
usage from most final consonants, the rule being to double it, if single, in inflexions & in some derivatives irrespective of the position of the accent.

1. When verbs in *-l* (except those in which a compound vowel sound, as
>>>

<<<
+ ai, ea, ee, oi, ow, ur, precedes the -l) make inflected or derived words in -able, -ed, -en, -er, or -ing, -ll- is written—controllable, carolled, befallen, traveller, equalling; but failed,
===
+ *ai, ea, ee, oi, ow, ur,* precedes the *-l*) make inflected or derived words in *-able, -ed, -en, -er,* or *-ing, -ll-* is written—*controllable, carolled, befallen, traveller, equalling*; but *failed,*
>>>

<<<
boiling, curled, &c., & before -ment l is not doubled; see also PARAL- LELED, WOOL,
===
*boiling, curled,* &c., & before *-ment* *l* is not doubled; see also ^^PARALLELED^^, ^^WOOL^^.
>>>

<<<
2. When nouns or adjectives in -l (with exceptions as in 1) make adjectives by addition of -ed, -er, or -y, the l is doubled: flannelled, jeweller, gravelly; but see UNPARAL- LELED.
===
2. When nouns or adjectives in *-l* (with exceptions as in 1) make adjectives by addition of *-ed, -er,* or *-y,* the *l* is doubled: *flannelled, jeweller, gravelly*; but see ^^UNPARALLELED^^.
>>>

<<<
Before -ish & -ism & -ist, l isnot doubled: devilish, liberalism, naturalist. Irregular superlatives vary, most using one l, but words in -ful always two: brutalest, loyalest, civil(ljest, joyfullest.
===
Before *-ish* & *-ism* & *-ist,* *l* is not doubled: *devilish, liberalism, naturalist.* Irregular superlatives vary, most using one *l,* but words in *-ful* always two: *brutalest, loyalest, civil(l)est, joyfullest.*
>>>

<<<
3. The simple form of a good many verbs vacillates between -l & -li, & no rule is possible that will secure the best form for all words & not conflict with strong usage for some; but it is perhaps safe to say that where such vacillation exists -ll is better if a precedes (appall, befall, enthrall, install),
===
3. The simple form of a good many verbs vacillates between *-l* & *-ll,* & no rule is possible that will secure the best form for all words & not conflict with strong usage for some; but it is perhaps safe to say that where such vacillation exists *-ll* is better if *a* precedes (*appall, befall, enthrall, install*),
>>>

<<<
& -l if another vowel, especially i (distil, instil, enrol, annul); verbs in -ll, however, take single l before -ment (enrolment, enthralment, instalment).
===
& *-l* if another vowel, especially *i* (*distil, instil, enrol, annul*); verbs in *-ll,* however, take single *l* before *-ment* (*enrolment, enthralment, instalment*).
>>>

<<<
4. Derivatives & compounds of words in -Il sometimes drop one l; so almighty, almost, already, altogether, always, but not alright; chilblain; fulfil; skilful; thraldom; wilful.
===
4. Derivatives & compounds of words in *-ll* sometimes drop one *l*; so *almighty, almost, already, altogether, always,* but not *alright*; *chilblain*; *fulfil*; *skilful*; *thraldom*; *wilful.*
>>>

<<<
This is perhaps helped by some apparent but not real examples such as belfry, bulrush, bulwark, & walnut, which are not from bell, bull, & wall.
===
This is perhaps helped by some apparent but not real examples such as *belfry, bulrush, bulwark,* & *walnut,* which are not from *bell, bull,* & *wall.*
>>>

<<<
Dul(l)ness & ful(l)ness are debatable; the older & much commoner spelling, but (according to the OED) the one less ‘in accordance with general analogies’ has only one l. Lloyd’s, underwriters.
===
*Dul(l)ness* & *ful(l)ness* are debatable; the older & much commoner spelling, but (according to the OED) the one less 'in accordance with general analogies' has only one *l.*

**Lloyd's,** underwriters.
>>>

<<<

So written, stone & star it is usual to spell load- ` not -ds or ds’. load, lode. In the compounds with stone, but lodestar. The first element is the same, & is the ordinary load, of which the original sense was way,
===
 So written, not *-ds* or *ds'*.

**load, lode.** In the compounds with *stone* & *star* it is usual to spell *loadstone,* but *lodestar.* The first element is the same, & is the ordinary *load,* of which the original sense was way,
